WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.170
- Hi everyone.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:14.310
I'm Maurice Samuels, the
director of the Yale program

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:15.980
for the study of antisemitism.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:19.230
And it's my great pleasure
to welcome you here today

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.780
to the next installment

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.980
in our Benjamin and Barbara
Zucker Lecture Series.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.450
Before I hand it over to
my colleague, Carolyn Dean

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:30.450
who will introduce today's speaker.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:31.720
I just want to say a word

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:34.380
about how it's going to work today.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.470
This is a webinar format

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:40.220
so the audience members
are not visible or audible

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.110
but we do want to hear from you.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:44.940
So if you have questions
and we hope you do

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.280
please submit them at any time

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.210
including during the talk itself,

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:53.320
using the Q and A function
at the bottom of your screen

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.630
don't use the chat and
don't raise your hand.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:58.900
We use the Q and A function only

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.310
at the bottom of the screen.

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.860
And then I will filter those questions

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:07.860
to Professor Ben-Ghiat
during the question section.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.240
So now without further ado

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.870
I will turn it over to my colleague.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:17.340
Carolyn Dean, who is the Charles J. Stille

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.010
Professor of History and French

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.943
at Yale University to
present today's speaker.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:24.320
- Thank you, Maurice.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.280
Welcome everyone.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.400
I'm delighted to have this opportunity

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.410
to introduce Professor Ruth Ben-Ghiat.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:36.080
Ruth Ben-Ghiat is a historian an educator

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.420
and a commentator on fascism,
authoritarian leaders

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.780
and propaganda and the
threats these presents

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.903
to democracies around the world.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.100
Professor, she is a professor
of history and Italian studies

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.490
at NYU or New York university.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:54.940
She's the author of seven
books and many essays

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:58.297
among them, I should say two
extremely important books,

00:01:58.297 --> 00:02:02.680
"Fascist Modernities: Italy, 1922-1945"

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:06.567
and "Italian Fascism's Empire Cinema".

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:13.450
Also an important second project
dealing with Italian empire

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.560
and she's published obviously much more,

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.150
including in CNN, The New
Yorker, the Washington Post.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.760
Now, she's here to discuss with us

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.267
themes from her newest book,

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:27.090
"Strongmen, Mussolini to the Present",

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.820
which just came out last year with Norton

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.940
and this the "Strongmen" examines

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.040
the authoritarian
playbook illiberal leaders

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.190
have used for a century.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:40.910
So with that, please join me in welcoming

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:42.423
Professor Ruth Ben-Ghiat.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:45.040
Thank you.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.820
Thank you to everyone
for coming and to Carolyn

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:53.010
and to Maurice Samuels
for inviting me to speak.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.680
So "Strongmen" comes out of my efforts

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:58.960
at civic education in a way,

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.020
it's a book written for the public.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:05.270
And so I want to start by
talking about why I wrote it.

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:09.070
And one of the reasons was a reaction

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:13.600
to a rather intense period
of revisionism of history

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.870
by the global right to
minimize the violence

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.020
of the authoritarian past.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:24.730
We see leaders, rehabilitating
autocratic predecessors

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.750
as Putin's putting up statues of Stalin

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:31.880
but Russian scholars who
write the wrong things

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.920
about Stalin's mass graves are imprisoned.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.230
You can't talk about the Nazi
Soviet pact in Russia anymore,

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:45.230
nor a Polish collaboration
with the Holocaust and Poland,

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.300
both scenario in Brazil and
many others on the global right

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:53.170
falsely claimed that fascism
was a left wing phenomenon.

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:56.420
And this has done of
course to tar the left

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:59.960
with fascist violence and leave
the right with clean hands.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.993
And this is a very common
talking point around the world.

00:04:05.688 --> 00:04:09.050
And when we see like a rally

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:12.530
that the far right proud boys held

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:15.870
in the States in September, 2020

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:18.030
I'll show you a slide later.

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:20.980
Not only you have the
Confederate flag, of course

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:24.087
but you have proud boys
wearing a t-shirt that says,

00:04:24.087 --> 00:04:26.040
"Pinochet did nothing wrong"

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:28.910
referring to the Chilean dictator.

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:32.350
So I really felt that to
understand what's going on today

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.830
including those who
spread hatred in America

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:38.830
we need a historical
and global perspective

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:41.193
on a century of authoritarianism.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.840
And of course, bringing this global lens

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:49.230
that historical lens to bear on America

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.940
was crucial to this book project.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.620
And it was attempt by me in American

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.920
to understand what was happening
to us under Donald Trump.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.370
And it's the first book
that came out in November

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:05.270
just after the election,
a week after the election.

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:08.440
It's the first book to
place the Trump presidency

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:12.460
in the context of a century
of authoritarian history.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.540
And I had written many things predicting

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:18.170
he would follow a kind
of authoritarian playbook

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:21.920
referring to 21st
century authoritarianism,

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.670
where you come to office via elections

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:28.220
and you manipulate elections
to try and stay there.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:31.129
And so it was intended
as a kind of warning

00:05:31.129 --> 00:05:35.550
that no country is immune
from the lure of leaders

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:39.035
who appear on the scene and
legitimize existing extremist

00:05:39.035 --> 00:05:42.547
and anti-democratic tendencies

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:48.320
and make a racism part of their platforms.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:51.070
And legitimate is also the idea

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:54.400
that violence is a way to cause problems

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.760
to deal with enemies of the state

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:59.190
and to move history forward.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:04.190
And we saw this coming to
a climax in January six.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:09.030
So the book is divided into three periods:

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:12.280
the fascist period, the
age of military coups

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.660
and what I call new authoritarianism

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:17.520
which starts with the end of the cold war.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:21.250
And I chose to focus mainly
on right-wing strongmen.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:23.280
So I'm just giving you the context now

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.800
in part, because of my
training and fascism,

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.250
and it was also interested
in individuals who damage

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:34.710
or destroy democracies rather
than kind of inherited power

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:37.120
as in China or North Korea

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:40.610
but clearly these right,
as in personality, cults

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:45.220
and tactics of violence,
the right and the left wing

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:48.990
authoritarian things unfold in dialogue.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.150
And I make that clear in the book.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:54.600
And the core of the book are chapters on

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.400
what I call the authoritarian playbook

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.626
the tools of rule and
propaganda corruption virility,

00:07:00.626 --> 00:07:04.220
the myth of national
greatness and violence.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:06.490
And each one goes over a hundred years

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:09.570
and this was quite
challenging to write it.

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:10.930
At times I had wished

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:14.300
I had just done a biographical
approach, you know

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.660
everything on Mussolini,
everything on Hitler

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:19.630
but I wanted the reader to be able to see

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:21.210
for example, with propaganda,

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:25.770
what changes and what stays
the same over a hundred years.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.650
And just to define my terms

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:31.580
I use the term strongmen
to refer to a leaders

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:34.310
who are a subset of authoritarians

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:35.900
who use all these tools

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:38.860
including masculinity or machismo

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:41.580
as a tool of political legitimacy.

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:44.010
So the virility is very important

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:46.770
in how they relate to their own people

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:50.580
and also in their relationships
with foreign despots

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:53.790
whether they actually wrecked democracy

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:56.560
or as happens in the 21st century,

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:00.053
kind of they're aspirational
and they damage it.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:04.330
So it's not a book of comparative politics

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:06.840
where I'm saying Trump is like Hitler,

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:10.800
but a history that traces through lines,

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.010
through a hundred years

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:15.030
like the fascist who fled to Franco Spain

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:17.600
and then went on to Pinochet's Chile,

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.920
and then on some of them
onto Berlusconi Italy

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:24.140
or the influential position

00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:28.170
of Opus Dei Catholics in
the governments of Franco,

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:30.860
Pinochet, Berlusconi, and Trump.

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:33.320
And of course, the groups that recur

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:36.600
as the strongmen's
victims: nomadic peoples

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:40.030
whether the Bedouin of
Libya or Roma people,

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:43.580
religious minorities, of
course, Jews and Muslims

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:47.560
and Jehovah's Witnesses
who are famously persecuted

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:49.870
by Hitler, but perhaps less known.

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:52.023
They're also persecuted by Putin.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:58.320
So against this backdrop, I want to talk

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:01.460
about the myth of national greatness

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:05.330
in particular the
contrast between this idea

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:09.310
of that the strongmen will
bring the nation to greatness

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:13.550
and the chaos and destruction
that these States have caused.

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:18.500
And it's really amazing how
the myth of authoritarianism

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:20.270
as efficient and productive,

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:22.370
the idea that the trains run on time

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:24.810
with Mussolini, it's really endured.

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:27.520
It's been hard to fell this myth

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:29.350
and or the idea that Franco

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:32.900
and Pinochet brought
stability to their countries.

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:36.960
And of course, antisemitism
recurs in this tragic history

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:40.200
of wastes of life, of through genocides

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:44.110
through loss of human and
financial resources of the state,

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:48.650
through killings, through
forced exiles, you know,

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:51.470
hundreds of thousands of
people going into exile

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:54.880
through capital flight and acts of plunder

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:58.480
against demonized categories
of people, you know

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:01.983
plundered bodies, plundered
bank accounts, and property.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:08.110
So I also want to talk
about personality cults

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:10.340
and the idea that the strongmen alone

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:13.150
can bring the nation to greatness

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:15.500
and the projects of national purification

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:17.200
that come with this.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:18.680
And some of these,

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:22.620
if you look at the big course
of a hundred years of history,

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:25.990
some of these are
counter-revolutionary projects

00:10:25.990 --> 00:10:27.710
against leftists.

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:31.020
So you have from the
fascist to the cold war

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:35.390
leaders like Pinochet up
through Bolsonaro and Trump.

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:37.790
And the rhetoric, for
example, of William Barr

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:40.540
who was attorney general until recently

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:43.600
with the police and the
way he talks about enemies

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:46.030
and on our case jurisdictions

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:49.640
that very much draws on this lineage.

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:53.020
And of course also perceived
racial and demographic

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:56.470
emergencies due to the
decline of white births

00:10:56.470 --> 00:11:00.730
and possible quote invasions
of the country by non-whites.

00:11:00.730 --> 00:11:04.400
This is something that recurs
with very similar rhetoric

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:09.400
from the fascist up to Orban
in Hungary and in Brazil today

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:12.530
among the global right.

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:16.300
And of course, where
demographic concerns surface

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:19.010
so does anti-Semitism.

00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:21.400
So I'm going to share my screen now

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:32.453
And just get rid of this.

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:38.960
And here's just a slide
from the proud boys rally

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:40.800
because I really think it's important

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:45.800
to keep this global reach of the far right

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:49.510
as it's manifesting in
America and American elsewhere

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:52.180
with antisemitism, with racism.

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:54.350
And it's too for us to forget

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:58.674
about the other side of things.

00:11:58.674 --> 00:12:01.180
And but it's all of a whole

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:02.680
and I think we need to see this

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:04.543
in more of a global the framework.

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:10.570
So one of the things that recurs is

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:13.620
why did these figures hold an appeal

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:15.030
at certain times in history?

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:19.890
And one recurrence that comes
over and over in the book

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:22.150
is that when countries have been through

00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:26.570
a period of a lot of
social immense pushing

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:28.460
could be gender equity, racial equity

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:31.700
it could be workers'
rights, secularization.

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:33.310
The times when male authority

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:35.410
and male status feel threatened.

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:39.150
This is when the idea of
the leader as omnipotent

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:42.950
and infallible compensates
for these anxieties.

00:12:42.950 --> 00:12:44.650
He is the anchor of the nation.

00:12:44.650 --> 00:12:49.300
He is the embodiment of the
aspiration to national purity.

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:52.250
And one dynamic and those of you familiar

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:55.640
with the fascist years,
this will be familiar.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:58.860
It's very important that the
strongman doesn't just embody,

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:01.020
he doesn't just represent the nation

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:04.410
like a democratic leader.

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:08.790
He embodies the nation,
he takes into himself

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:12.290
the sorrows, the victim
hood of the nation.

00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:15.040
So he physically embodies it.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:16.663
And this is an interesting,

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:22.790
this is by pro Trump
painter, Jon McNaughton

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:24.340
called "Respect the Flag".

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:28.810
And it's about the
controversy over NFL kneeling

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:32.680
to protest police racism and brutality.

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:36.130
But what I would like you to see here

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:39.360
is the way that Trump has depicted,

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:41.440
he's quiet he's stricken.

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:44.724
He shows emotions that indeed the

00:13:44.724 --> 00:13:49.030
famously non-empathetic
Trump didn't really have,

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:52.190
but he's clutching the
tattered flag to himself.

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:54.850
Again, he's full of sorrow.

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:59.210
And so he represents the
emotions that the nation perhaps

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:01.390
can't express for itself.

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:05.410
And so all of these figures
are able to, this was of course

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:08.390
famously said of Hitler, that
when he would give speeches

00:14:08.390 --> 00:14:11.950
he was expressing things that people felt

00:14:11.950 --> 00:14:14.980
they didn't know how
to express themselves.

00:14:14.980 --> 00:14:19.980
And this becomes important
when minorities are persecuted

00:14:20.840 --> 00:14:25.453
and the leader is the embodiment
of kind of the good nation.

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:32.570
Here's another to do with the
fantasies of national purity.

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:35.240
So of course they are
acclaimed from below.

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:38.150
They are there by the will of the people.

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:40.110
And this is true, whether they're elected

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:42.000
or whether as in Qaddafi's case,

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.576
they come in by military coup.

00:14:44.576 --> 00:14:49.576
But they also are getting
inspiration from divine sources.

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:54.330
So here is a classic, this
is both these phenomenon

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:56.640
are represented in this billboard.

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:58.720
It's with you, we embrace glory.

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:00.780
This is the people telling Gaddafi

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:03.780
from below that he elevates them.

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:06.441
And yet he's looking heavenward

00:15:06.441 --> 00:15:10.240
and Gaddafi used to stop in
the middle of his speeches

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:12.870
and look up to the heavens

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:15.473
as though he was getting
divine inspiration.

00:15:16.870 --> 00:15:21.490
So strongmen national purity projects

00:15:21.490 --> 00:15:25.580
usually leverage three
separate timeframes.

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:29.080
One is a utopia and of course

