So I think I'll start with this
question from the audience,

00:32:14.477 --> 00:32:18.300
"As you describe it, your
book emphasizes the continuity

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:21.470
between strongmen from
Mussolini to the present.

00:32:21.470 --> 00:32:23.990
Can you also talk about how they differ?

00:32:23.990 --> 00:32:26.330
I'm thinking of the frequent mention today

00:32:26.330 --> 00:32:28.180
of liberal democracies

00:32:28.180 --> 00:32:31.630
or sophisticated autocrats
who rely on the vocabulary

00:32:31.630 --> 00:32:34.877
of democracy without
respecting the thing itself?"

00:32:36.360 --> 00:32:37.990
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:32:37.990 --> 00:32:39.680
And one of the...

00:32:39.680 --> 00:32:43.800
I felt it was really important to go back

00:32:43.800 --> 00:32:47.560
and look back over a century
of authoritarian history,

00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:52.560
because when people the
word fascism is back

00:32:53.100 --> 00:32:57.160
people are comparing both
scenario or Trump to fascists.

00:32:57.160 --> 00:33:00.090
And as a historian, I
really wanted to go back

00:33:00.090 --> 00:33:05.090
and give each specific period it's due,

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:07.020
look at the through lines

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:10.230
but really respect those differences in.

00:33:10.230 --> 00:33:13.100
So the whole first part of the
book is how they get to power

00:33:13.100 --> 00:33:14.520
and each period.

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:17.120
So, you know, fascist era is one thing.

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:19.700
Military coups is a
totally different thing.

00:33:19.700 --> 00:33:22.870
So today I make it really clear and also

00:33:22.870 --> 00:33:26.277
in every chapter that I call
it new authoritarianism.

00:33:26.277 --> 00:33:31.277
And this is where you come to
power, mostly by elections.

00:33:31.340 --> 00:33:35.220
You manipulate elections to
stay there and information.

00:33:35.220 --> 00:33:37.040
For example, with propaganda

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:39.670
it's really interesting
how much stays the same

00:33:39.670 --> 00:33:42.180
like the cannons personality cults

00:33:42.180 --> 00:33:44.630
they really haven't changed,

00:33:44.630 --> 00:33:48.141
but of course, social
media and kind of soups up

00:33:48.141 --> 00:33:53.141
and accelerates everything
to do with propaganda

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:57.950
because propaganda works
on saturation on repetition

00:33:57.950 --> 00:34:00.350
on the same message with tiny variations.

00:34:00.350 --> 00:34:03.533
And this is what social media does best.

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:05.960
So there's that tension

00:34:05.960 --> 00:34:08.670
between what is different
and what is the same.

00:34:08.670 --> 00:34:12.420
And another thing is the
way that there's a chapter

00:34:12.420 --> 00:34:16.790
on resistance and the way
that resistance is practiced

00:34:16.790 --> 00:34:19.860
is also helped by social media today.

00:34:19.860 --> 00:34:22.861
So that for example, the classic image

00:34:22.861 --> 00:34:25.790
from other eras of authoritarianism

00:34:25.790 --> 00:34:28.890
of the police banging down,
they're banging on the door

00:34:28.890 --> 00:34:32.080
of the resistor and the
resistor has to hide

00:34:32.080 --> 00:34:35.460
his or her documents, right?

00:34:35.460 --> 00:34:37.230
In the past we wouldn't see this,

00:34:37.230 --> 00:34:41.300
we'd hear about it in memoirs or exiles

00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:46.080
now in Putin's Russia, for
example, it's, live-streamed

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:49.180
by the resistor and they
create these communities

00:34:49.180 --> 00:34:53.520
of empathy and one
dissident, he live streams

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:56.820
on Twitter he videoed his hard drive,

00:34:56.820 --> 00:34:59.290
being flown out by a drone.

00:34:59.290 --> 00:35:01.860
He sent his drone to his friend's house.

00:35:01.860 --> 00:35:04.730
And so on Twitter, everybody
watched the drone leave

00:35:04.730 --> 00:35:07.260
with his hard drive,
carrying it to safety.

00:35:07.260 --> 00:35:11.433
So that's an example of how things change.

00:35:12.370 --> 00:35:15.590
- Great. Carolyn, I don't
know if you want to jump in.

00:35:15.590 --> 00:35:16.460
- I actually do.

00:35:16.460 --> 00:35:19.309
I kind of have a follow-up to that,

00:35:19.309 --> 00:35:23.003
that question which you
answered so clearly.

00:35:24.290 --> 00:35:28.700
A larger framework in a
smaller one really briefly.

00:35:28.700 --> 00:35:31.710
You talked about the
importance of efficiency

00:35:31.710 --> 00:35:34.773
trains running on time obviously
Hitler built the Autobahn

00:35:34.773 --> 00:35:35.796
and this kind of thing.

00:35:35.796 --> 00:35:37.046
And it seems...

00:35:38.150 --> 00:35:39.770
and obviously this coexists

00:35:39.770 --> 00:35:42.230
with this new kind of media
presence, et cetera, et cetera

00:35:42.230 --> 00:35:43.670
that these strongmen have

00:35:43.670 --> 00:35:44.920
but it strikes me

00:35:45.861 --> 00:35:49.130
that the signature of
authoritarian leaders,

00:35:49.130 --> 00:35:53.002
at least in, say, I'd
begin, Bolsonaro or Trump

00:35:53.002 --> 00:35:58.002
is kind of a, this chaos and anarchy

00:35:58.730 --> 00:36:01.940
and I kind of wondered how
you make sense of that.

00:36:01.940 --> 00:36:05.350
And then really briefly
the larger framework again,

00:36:05.350 --> 00:36:07.340
is it, you also, you pointed out

00:36:07.340 --> 00:36:10.700
that this all really takes
off in, I presume the '90s

00:36:10.700 --> 00:36:15.270
at the end of the cold
war with these so-called

00:36:15.270 --> 00:36:18.090
triumph of liberal capitalist democracy,

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:20.390
whereas fascism in the '20s and '30s

00:36:20.390 --> 00:36:23.930
is clearly born of the
collapse of liberal,

00:36:23.930 --> 00:36:27.147
whatever version of liberal
capitalism existed then.

00:36:27.147 --> 00:36:32.147
And I wondered if how
that, if that's important,

00:36:34.027 --> 00:36:39.027
this is in this strain
of difference, I guess.

00:36:39.550 --> 00:36:44.550
- Yeah, one of the, the
macro themes of the book

00:36:45.170 --> 00:36:50.170
was to bust this myth of
authoritarian efficiency.

00:36:52.540 --> 00:36:56.540
And I wanted to...

00:36:56.540 --> 00:36:58.360
throughout the book in each chapter

00:36:58.360 --> 00:37:02.410
I focus on the chaos
on the destructiveness.

00:37:02.410 --> 00:37:06.940
So what we know, for example,
from those who study,

00:37:06.940 --> 00:37:10.100
who know the fascist antisemitism,

00:37:10.100 --> 00:37:11.660
the plundering of assets,

00:37:11.660 --> 00:37:13.638
how Jews had to give up their assets,

00:37:13.638 --> 00:37:16.100
the state seize their assets, right,

00:37:16.100 --> 00:37:20.610
and gave them, you know, very
little back or nothing at all.

00:37:20.610 --> 00:37:25.610
This is one phase in a
plundering of business,

00:37:26.170 --> 00:37:29.070
so we hear that authoritarian
is great for business, right,

00:37:29.070 --> 00:37:30.950
because it brings stability.

00:37:30.950 --> 00:37:34.750
Well, it's very good for
certain big business,

00:37:34.750 --> 00:37:37.310
if you're in the oil industry or weapons,

00:37:37.310 --> 00:37:41.240
like the sectors that
strongmen care about,

00:37:41.240 --> 00:37:42.550
it's good for you.

00:37:42.550 --> 00:37:45.270
Like with Trump, Big Pharma mining,

00:37:45.270 --> 00:37:47.260
but business as a whole

00:37:47.260 --> 00:37:50.750
and small business has been decimated

00:37:50.750 --> 00:37:53.670
over and over again around
the world by strongmen.

00:37:53.670 --> 00:37:54.680
And that's very important

00:37:54.680 --> 00:37:56.610
and we don't hear enough about that.

00:37:56.610 --> 00:37:59.610
So Putin, for example has jailed

00:37:59.610 --> 00:38:02.380
tens of thousands of businessmen

00:38:02.380 --> 00:38:05.910
because if your company does
too well, he goes after it

00:38:05.910 --> 00:38:08.820
and it's this phenomenon
known as predation,

00:38:08.820 --> 00:38:11.530
predator, you know, to be a predator.

00:38:11.530 --> 00:38:16.120
So authoritarian States not
surprising our predatory States.

00:38:16.120 --> 00:38:19.220
And we don't, we hear about the violence

00:38:19.220 --> 00:38:22.790
of the predatory violence
and what they do to bodies

00:38:22.790 --> 00:38:24.520
locking them up and killing them.

00:38:24.520 --> 00:38:26.720
We don't hear what they do to capital.

00:38:26.720 --> 00:38:30.420
We don't hear how they ruin, you know

00:38:30.420 --> 00:38:35.110
family businesses that have
been there for decades, right,

00:38:35.110 --> 00:38:38.830
for generations and the same
is true in Erdogan's, Turkey.

00:38:38.830 --> 00:38:42.450
We're over $32 billion worth of assets

00:38:42.450 --> 00:38:46.340
and schools and businesses
have been seized by Erdogan,

00:38:46.340 --> 00:38:48.300
so this is part of it.

00:38:48.300 --> 00:38:49.500
The other thing just quickly

00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:53.070
is their governance
structure is highly chaotic

00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:55.900
with constant recycling of people.

00:38:55.900 --> 00:38:58.170
Even like somebody like Pinochet,

00:38:58.170 --> 00:39:00.760
who seemed like, the famous picture of him

00:39:00.760 --> 00:39:03.330
with the dark glasses,
he seemed statuesque,

00:39:03.330 --> 00:39:05.250
he seemed stable.

00:39:05.250 --> 00:39:09.070
He reshuffled his government
like over 40 times.

00:39:09.070 --> 00:39:11.680
So, because nobody's loyal enough,

00:39:11.680 --> 00:39:13.870
because of the same things
we've lived through with Trump,

00:39:13.870 --> 00:39:18.870
this is classic, it's constant chaos.

00:39:19.610 --> 00:39:23.690
And in fact, some of them
like Gaddafi and Idi Amin,

00:39:23.690 --> 00:39:26.740
they really, psychologically
they thrived on chaos

00:39:26.740 --> 00:39:29.130
and chaos is a psychological weapon

00:39:29.130 --> 00:39:32.810
in all these States, whether
they wreck democracy or not,

00:39:32.810 --> 00:39:36.160
it's constant scandal, constant outrage,

00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:38.570
constant chaos of one form or another

00:39:38.570 --> 00:39:41.010
and it's exhausting to people,

00:39:41.010 --> 00:39:45.660
even if their life's not at
risk as in a true dictatorship.

00:39:45.660 --> 00:39:48.280
It's exhausting and authoritarianism plays

00:39:48.280 --> 00:39:50.963
on us being exhausted from the chaos.

00:39:52.402 --> 00:39:57.402
- So interesting and here's
a question from the audience

00:39:57.530 --> 00:40:01.350
and something that I think
has occurred to a lot of us,

00:40:01.350 --> 00:40:05.690
Trump famously doesn't read,
he's not a student of history,

00:40:05.690 --> 00:40:07.630
so where does he...

00:40:07.630 --> 00:40:09.487
how did he so perfectly follow

00:40:09.487 --> 00:40:14.330
all of this authoritarian models,

00:40:14.330 --> 00:40:18.963
is it instinctual for him
or how did he do this?

00:40:19.810 --> 00:40:21.500
- Yeah, it's so interesting

00:40:21.500 --> 00:40:23.431
because he really does follow it.

00:40:23.431 --> 00:40:28.431
I mean, I was able to predict
almost everything he did

00:40:28.760 --> 00:40:32.170
and the GOP's response,
not because I'm psychic

00:40:32.170 --> 00:40:34.740
but because I have studied this stuff

00:40:34.740 --> 00:40:37.790
and I'm always upset that I'm right.

00:40:37.790 --> 00:40:39.240
I would love to be wrong

00:40:39.240 --> 00:40:41.450
but I haven't been wrong, unfortunately.

00:40:41.450 --> 00:40:43.580
And this is because number one,

00:40:43.580 --> 00:40:46.170
they have similar personalities.

00:40:46.170 --> 00:40:48.661
And so they structure their governance

00:40:48.661 --> 00:40:53.203
in similar ways with which
produces similar results.

00:40:54.130 --> 00:40:57.450
So for example, all of them
have these inner sanctums,

00:40:57.450 --> 00:41:01.730
I call them also cocoons with
sycophants and family members,

00:41:01.730 --> 00:41:03.670
it's very important to have sons in law,

00:41:03.670 --> 00:41:06.235
I have a whole paragraphs on sons-in-law

00:41:06.235 --> 00:41:09.300
from Ciano, Mussolini up to Jared Kushner

00:41:09.300 --> 00:41:13.860
and these are because they
can't have any proper feedback.

00:41:13.860 --> 00:41:16.210
So that's one example.

00:41:16.210 --> 00:41:19.890
Also the impulsiveness, the
chaos, the vindictiveness

00:41:21.312 --> 00:41:22.810
all of that is the same

00:41:22.810 --> 00:41:25.610
and so it leads to similar outcomes.

00:41:25.610 --> 00:41:28.100
Now, Trump is indeed, he is different

00:41:28.100 --> 00:41:31.083
than everybody who came before
him because he doesn't read.

00:41:32.050 --> 00:41:33.680
So of course there...

00:41:33.680 --> 00:41:35.690
but none of them, there is no manual

00:41:35.690 --> 00:41:38.950
although they do watch
what each other does.

00:41:38.950 --> 00:41:41.250
So Hitler learned from Mussolini,

00:41:41.250 --> 00:41:43.760
he studied him very closely

00:41:43.760 --> 00:41:48.360
and they also watch very
carefully how they fall.

00:41:48.360 --> 00:41:53.360
So when Gaddafi saw what
happened to Saddam Hussein

00:41:53.440 --> 00:41:56.050
he actually changed his policies

00:41:56.050 --> 00:41:58.997
and cooperated with the
West because he said,

00:41:58.997 --> 00:42:02.330
"I'm not going to end up
being dragged out of a hole."

00:42:02.330 --> 00:42:05.000
Which is actually what happened to him.

00:42:05.000 --> 00:42:07.810
But then when that happened to him,

00:42:07.810 --> 00:42:12.190
Putin became very
obsessed with Arab spring

00:42:12.190 --> 00:42:14.550
and he watched over and over again

00:42:14.550 --> 00:42:17.530
the footage of Gaddafi being killed.

00:42:17.530 --> 00:42:20.773
And he then had his own crackdown, 2012.

00:42:22.360 --> 00:42:25.120
He started, he came back to the presidency

00:42:25.120 --> 00:42:27.960
but he really cracked down
and he had mass protests.

00:42:27.960 --> 00:42:30.720
But this was also because he was worried

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:33.840
that Arab spring would
spell the end for him.

00:42:33.840 --> 00:42:38.820
So it's instinct, it's
personality similarities

00:42:38.820 --> 00:42:43.503
but it's also, there is
some monitoring going on.

00:42:44.856 --> 00:42:49.540
- So interesting, so here's a
question from our colleague,

00:42:49.540 --> 00:42:53.880
Sam Moines who says, "This brilliant Ruth,

00:42:53.880 --> 00:42:56.220
but I'm wondering how we avoid sweeping

00:42:56.220 --> 00:42:58.220
with too broad a brush

00:42:58.220 --> 00:43:01.430
and stigmatizing legitimate,
or even uplifting

00:43:01.430 --> 00:43:03.800
political arguments or worse,

00:43:03.800 --> 00:43:07.550
for example, it has to be
legitimate to call out elites

00:43:07.550 --> 00:43:11.330
and I've never seen a more
utopian moment in this country

00:43:11.330 --> 00:43:14.370
than when Barack Obama was
running, but I'm left unsure

00:43:14.370 --> 00:43:16.600
whether you think such
things are too dangerous

00:43:16.600 --> 00:43:20.207
given scary talk of cabals or
right-wing utopian discourse."

00:43:23.150 --> 00:43:26.120
- For the detailed response, read the book

00:43:26.120 --> 00:43:29.960
but calling for good
example, calling out leads.

00:43:29.960 --> 00:43:33.970
So, because they're also different kinds,

00:43:33.970 --> 00:43:36.990
I don't use the word populism too much,

00:43:36.990 --> 00:43:41.990
because populism is an
ideology used by Mussolini

00:43:42.330 --> 00:43:44.550
and it's there are populous parties

00:43:44.550 --> 00:43:47.920
but a lot of the authoritarians
are not populous.

00:43:47.920 --> 00:43:50.850
Like Pinochet was not
a populist for example,

00:43:50.850 --> 00:43:54.710
but calling out elites
can be very solitary,

00:43:54.710 --> 00:43:59.440
can be very healthy,
except when authoritarians

00:43:59.440 --> 00:44:03.350
and a lot of these populous
parties are doing it today,

00:44:03.350 --> 00:44:08.350
it's a foe calling out because
in fact one of the biggest

00:44:08.370 --> 00:44:13.370
hypocrisy that exists is the
idea of the anti globalist,

00:44:15.430 --> 00:44:17.330
that whole rhetoric of anti globalist,

00:44:17.330 --> 00:44:20.870
because who is a bigger
globalist than Trump.

00:44:20.870 --> 00:44:22.060
His whole business model

00:44:22.060 --> 00:44:25.902
was licensing his name all over the world.

00:44:25.902 --> 00:44:30.060
He and Ivanka even today
have Chinese trademarks

00:44:30.060 --> 00:44:33.940
or Putin and think of the post-communist

00:44:33.940 --> 00:44:37.810
version of anti globalism, which
involves calling out elites

00:44:37.810 --> 00:44:39.060
Western elites.

00:44:39.060 --> 00:44:40.370
They all have their money,

00:44:40.370 --> 00:44:42.610
their illicitly gained money stored

00:44:42.610 --> 00:44:47.610
in offshore finance networks,
which are mostly in the West.

00:44:47.970 --> 00:44:51.150
So these are the biggest
globalists that there are.

00:44:51.150 --> 00:44:56.150
So, this is why the kind
of toolbox is important

00:44:58.160 --> 00:45:00.900
because propaganda or rhetorical themes

00:45:00.900 --> 00:45:03.870
such as anti-elitism,

00:45:03.870 --> 00:45:05.680
when they're married with corruption

00:45:05.680 --> 00:45:07.400
which is what I've been talking about

00:45:07.400 --> 00:45:12.070
with ex filtrating assets, like Putin does

00:45:12.070 --> 00:45:14.283
to globalist havens.

00:45:15.800 --> 00:45:17.900
When you see it in that frame

00:45:17.900 --> 00:45:20.040
and you don't take only one of them,

00:45:20.040 --> 00:45:23.500
when you keep them in
their kind of context,

00:45:23.500 --> 00:45:27.010
then there's nothing salutary about that.

00:45:27.010 --> 00:45:28.140
That's just corruption

00:45:28.140 --> 00:45:30.623
and that's the basis of authoritarianism.

00:45:32.868 --> 00:45:36.024
- So a question from another
colleague, from Katie Truppner,

00:45:36.024 --> 00:45:38.410
who'd like to hear more
about the historic links

00:45:38.410 --> 00:45:43.370
between Mussolini and Hitlerian
fascism and imperialism.

00:45:43.370 --> 00:45:48.050
And also whether you see
similar geopolitical aspirations

00:45:48.050 --> 00:45:50.750
among strongmen today,

00:45:50.750 --> 00:45:53.880
like the revanchist
nostalgia for lost empires,

00:45:53.880 --> 00:45:56.580
like Greater Hungary
or the Ottoman empire,

00:45:56.580 --> 00:45:57.483
things like that.

00:46:00.750 --> 00:46:05.750
- So for the first part of
Italian fascism and imperialism,

00:46:09.790 --> 00:46:14.790
so, we actually don't know know enough

00:46:17.220 --> 00:46:21.170
about the global reach of Italian fascism,

00:46:21.170 --> 00:46:24.890
but what is very interesting
is that the model of empire

00:46:24.890 --> 00:46:29.890
that Mussolini was propagating
was a little bit different

00:46:31.430 --> 00:46:36.430
because Mussolini wanted
to do territorial expansion

00:46:36.620 --> 00:46:39.980
and he did, eventually in
the Balkans in World War II

00:46:39.980 --> 00:46:44.720
but he also wanted to
recuperate for the empire

00:46:44.720 --> 00:46:46.800
the little Italy's around the world,

00:46:46.800 --> 00:46:51.391
this kind of soft power, soft imperialism

00:46:51.391 --> 00:46:53.583
that involved the diaspora.

00:46:54.550 --> 00:46:57.220
And in this, as in so many other things,

00:46:57.220 --> 00:47:00.800
Mussolini is linked to better than Hitler

00:47:00.800 --> 00:47:05.800
to 21st century authoritarians,
such as Modi or Duterte

00:47:06.140 --> 00:47:07.380
who make a big...

00:47:07.380 --> 00:47:09.560
they're not invading territory

00:47:09.560 --> 00:47:11.690
'cause that's less common today,

00:47:11.690 --> 00:47:14.530
but they are using a diaspora

00:47:14.530 --> 00:47:16.560
to have a sense of a greater nation.

00:47:16.560 --> 00:47:17.970
In fact, the chapter in the book

00:47:17.970 --> 00:47:20.300
is called 'A Greater Nation.'

00:47:20.300 --> 00:47:24.150
And it deals with these themes of exile,

00:47:24.150 --> 00:47:27.943
these themes of calling
out to the diaspora,

00:47:29.030 --> 00:47:34.030
as well as you have an Erdogan
who's like, aggressive,

00:47:35.080 --> 00:47:38.980
and de-stabilizing because of his desire.

00:47:38.980 --> 00:47:42.730
